To build a web application that displays the current time in different countries, you can follow these steps:

1. **Choose a Technology Stack**: Decide on the programming languages and frameworks you will use. For example, you could use JavaScript (or TypeScript) along with HTML and CSS, or a server-side language like Python with a framework such as Flask or Django.

2. **Fetch Time Data**: You need a way to get the current time for each country. One approach is to use an API that provides time zone information. The `moment-timezone` library for JavaScript or libraries like `pytz` for Python can be useful here.

3. **Create the User Interface**: Design a simple interface where users can select countries or see a list of countries with their respective current times displayed.

4. **Implement the Logic**: Write the logic to fetch the time data based on the selected countries and update the UI accordingly.

5. **Testing**: Ensure the application works correctly across different time zones and devices.

6. **Deployment**: Deploy your application to a web server so it can be accessed by users.

Here’s a basic example using JavaScript and HTML:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <title>World Clock</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>World Clock</h1>
    <select id="countrySelect">
        <!-- Options for countries -->
    </select>
    <div id="timeDisplay"></div>

    <script>
        document.addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded", function() {
            const timeDisplay = document.getElementById('timeDisplay');
            const countrySelect = document.getElementById('countrySelect');

            // Populate the dropdown with countries
            ['USA', 'India', 'Japan'].forEach(country => {
                let option = document.createElement('option');
                option.value = country;
                option.textContent = country;
                countrySelect.appendChild(option);
            });

            // Function to update time
            function updateTime(country) {
                const timezone = country + '/UTC';
                moment.tz.setDefault(timezone);
                const currentTime = moment().format('HH:mm:ss ZZ');
                timeDisplay.textContent = `${country}: ${currentTime}`;
            }

            // Update time when page loads and on selection change
            updateTime(countrySelect.value);
            countrySelect.addEventListener('change', function() {
                updateTime(this.value);
            });
        });
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

This is a simplified example to give you an idea of how to proceed. Depending on your requirements, you might want to integrate more features or use more sophisticated tools and APIs.

