To retrieve data from a web server using Node.js, you can use the built-in `http` module or popular libraries like `axios` or `fetch`.

Here's a basic example using the `http` module to make an HTTP GET request:

```javascript
const http = require('http');

http.get('http://example.com/api/data', (response) => {
  let data = '';

  // A chunk of data has been received.
  response.on('data', (chunk) => {
    data += chunk;
  });

  // The whole response has been received. Print out the result.
  response.on('end', () => {
    console.log(JSON.parse(data));
  });
}).on("error", (err) => {
  console.log("Error: " + err.message);
});
```

Alternatively, using `axios`, which is easier and more flexible:

```javascript
const axios = require('axios');

axios.get('http://example.com/api/data')
  .then(response => {
    console.log(JSON.stringify(response.data));
  })
  .catch(error => {
    console.error("Error:", error);
  });
```

Make sure to install `axios` via npm if you haven't already:

```bash
npm install axios
```

Choose the method that best fits your needs based on the complexity and requirements of your project.

