continuation:
```java
import java.lang.reflect.Field;

public class MemorySizeExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        List<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>();
        list.add(3);
        list.add(4);
        list.add(5);

        // Getting the memory size of the list
        Field field = list.getClass().getDeclaredField("elementData");
        field.setAccessible(true);
        Object elementData = field.get(list);

        long size = Runtime.getRuntime().totalMemory() - Runtime.getRuntime().freeMemory();
        long elementSize = elementData instanceof int[] ? 4 : 8; // Size of int or Integer

        long totalSize = ((int[]) elementData).length * elementSize;
        System.out.println("Memory size of the list: " + totalSize + " bytes");
    }
}
```
Note that this approach is not accurate and is more of an approximation because the actual memory usage can vary based on JVM implementation details and other factors. For precise memory measurement, you might want to use profiling tools or libraries designed for such tasks.

